    <desc>Create, edit, copy, export and delete profiles, and choose the default one.</desc>
  </info>

  <title>Manage profiles</title>

<p>A profile is a set of settings for terminals: their font and colors,
  their size, their scrolling, the command they run and more. You can have
  several, for example one to <link xref="pref-custom-command">connect to
  another computer</link> with <cmd>ssh</cmd>, and choose one for each
  terminal.</p>

  <p>The profiles are listed in the <gui>Profiles</gui> group of the
  preferences. To open them, press the menu button in the top-right corner of
  the window and select <gui style="menuitem">Preferences</gui>.</p>

  <section id="select-profile">
    <title>Use a profile</title>
    <p>To open a tab with another profile, click the arrow next to the
    <gui style="button">New Tab</gui> button in the header bar and select the
    profile. To change the profile of the current terminal, right-click it and
    choose one in <gui style="menuitem">Profiles</gui>. The
    <link xref="cmd-palette">command palette</link> offers both too.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="new-profile">
    <title>Create a profile</title>
    <p>Click the <gui style="button">+</gui> row below the profiles. A profile
    named <gui>New Profile</gui> with the default settings is added; click it
    to edit it.</p>
    <p>To create a profile that starts with the settings of another one, click
    the menu button of that profile and select
    <gui style="menuitem">Clone</gui>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="edit-profile">
    <title>Edit a profile</title>
    <p>Click a profile to show its settings on a page of their own. Changes are
    saved at once. The name of the profile is at the top of the page; edit it
    and press the apply button to rename the profile.</p>
    <p>There you can choose the <link xref="app-fonts">font</link>, the
    <link xref="app-colors">colors</link>, the
    <link xref="app-cursor">cursor</link>, the
    <link xref="app-terminal-sizes">initial size</link>, the
    <link xref="pref-scrolling">scrolling</link>, the
    <link xref="pref-bell">bell</link>, the
    <link xref="pref-custom-command">command</link> and what happens
    <link xref="pref-custom-exit">when it exits</link>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="export">
    <title>Export and import a profile</title>
    <p>To take a profile to another computer, click its menu button, select
    <gui style="menuitem">Export…</gui> and save it to a
    <file>.pano-profile</file> file. On the other computer, click
    <gui style="button">Import…</gui> above the list of profiles and choose
    the file. The file holds the settings that differ from the defaults.</p>
    <p>To copy the profiles of <app>GNOME Terminal</app>, see
    <link xref="gs-import"/>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="delete-profile">
    <title>Delete a profile</title>
    <p>Click the menu button of the profile and select
    <gui style="menuitem">Delete</gui>. The default profile cannot be deleted;
    make another profile the default first.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="set-default">
    <title>Set the default profile</title>
    <p>The default profile is used when <app>Pano Terminal</app> cannot take
    the profile of another terminal, for example for the first window after it
    starts. New tabs and windows opened from a terminal get the profile of
    that terminal.</p>
    <p>To make a profile the default, click its menu button and select
    <gui style="menuitem">Set as default</gui>.</p>
  </section>

